+++
title: Duat
description: Underworld in Egyptian mythology
hook: The worst thing that could happen to an ancient Egyptian in the Duat was
  not fire. It was a heart heavier than a feather, eaten by Ammit, and then
  simply not existing any more.
standfirst: "Ancient Egypt's underworld, known only from funerary texts and
  never depicted the same way twice: a dark country of gates, rivers and lakes
  of fire, where the sun god sails through the twelve hours of the night and
  the dead are weighed against a feather."
key-facts:
  - name: What it is
    value: Underworld in Egyptian mythology — a concept involving death, most
      often seen as a realm where people go after they die
  - name: Also called
    value: "Tuat; Te (Coptic: Ⲧⲏ, romanized: Tē); Amenthes (Ancient Greek:
      Ἀμένθης, romanized: Aménthēs)"
  - name: Where it belongs
    value: Ancient Egypt, and the religion of ancient Egypt
  - name: Its lord
    value: Osiris, god of the Duat and personification of rebirth
pull-quotes: []
timeline:
  - when: around 1275 BCE
    what: The Papyrus of Hunefer, whose 'Weighing of the Heart' scene shows the
      judgment of the dead in the presence of Osiris, is dated to the 19th
      Dynasty.
  - when: 1885–1890
    what: The Duat is described in Meyers Konversations-Lexikon, 4th edition.
  - when: "2022"
    what: The Duat appears in the episode 'Asylum' of the Disney+ series Moon
      Knight.
image-plan:
  - file: The_judgement_of_the_dead_in_the_presence_of_Osiris.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: The whole judgment in one strip, from the Papyrus of Hunefer, dated
      to the 19th Dynasty around 1275 BCE. Anubis takes the dead Hunefer into
      the judgment hall and weighs the portion of his soul represented by his
      heart; Ammit the Devourer waits on the result and Thoth records it. At
      the right Hunefer has passed, and the falcon-headed Horus presents him
      to Osiris, enthroned with Isis, Nephthys and the four sons of Horus.
  - file: Coffin_of_Gua.jpg
    role: inline
    section: overview
    caption: "The Book of Two Ways, a Coffin Text, is about as close to a map of
      the Duat as Egyptian writing gets: the two pathways run across the right
      middle of this coffin."
  - file: BD_Field_of_Hotep.jpg
    role: full
    section: geography
    caption: A'aru, 'The Field of Reeds', from the Papyrus of Ani — the paradise
      waiting for the dead who are found virtuous.
  - file: Ani_chap125.jpg
    role: inline
    section: housing-of-souls-and-afterlife
    caption: The 42 Judges of Ma'at, the jury at the 'Weighing of the Heart'. The
      dead had to address each of the forty-two by name.
  - file: Book_of_Gates_Barque_of_Ra_cropped.jpg
    role: full
    section: journey-of-the-sun
    caption: "Af, or Afu, commonly known as Afu-Ra: the ram-headed form of Ra
      travelling the Duat on the subterrestrial Nile — the 12 hours of night
      and the underworld — on the Mesektet barque, with Sia in front of him
      and Heka behind, inside the coils of the protective serpent deity
      Mehen."
  - file: The_Weighing_of_the_Heart.svg
    role: inline
    section: differences-in-sources
    caption: "The Weighing of the Heart in the Duat, from a papyrus of the
      Egyptian Book of the Dead: Anubis at the far right, the feather
      balancing the scales, and Ammit waiting for the hearts that need
      devouring."
glossary:
  - term: Duat
    gloss: "The Egyptian underworld: a dark subterranean realm that houses the
      deceased and a variety of deities, most often seen as where people go
      after they die."
  - term: Shetit
    gloss: The name most of the Duat's dark, gated lands go by, translated as 'The
      Beyond' and described as dangerous.
  - term: A'aru
    gloss: "'The Field of Reeds', the paradise the virtuous dead may dwell in,
      where harvests are never poor and the land is in eternal spring."
  - term: Weighing of the Heart
    gloss: The judgment ritual in which Anubis weighs the dead person's heart
      against the feather of Maat, and Thoth records the verdict.
  - term: Assessors of Maat
    gloss: The forty-two judges who sit as jury at the weighing; the dead had to
      address each by name and recite the sins they did not commit.
  - term: Ammit
    gloss: The devourer of souls, crocodile-faced, lion in front and hippopotamus
      behind, who eats the hearts of those who fail the weighing.
  - term: Af (Afu)
    gloss: The ram-headed form Ra takes while travelling through the Duat at night.
  - term: Amduat
    gloss: A funerary text; by its account the underworld has twelve regions, one
      for each hour of the sun god's night journey.
further:
  - slug: book-of-the-dead
    why: The text this article leans on hardest — also known as The Book of Coming
      Forth by Day, and one of the most studied in Egyptology.
  - slug: osiris
    why: Lord of the Duat, killed by his brother Seth and partially revived by
      Isis; the realm's rebirth runs through him.
  - slug: ancient-egyptian-afterlife-beliefs
    why: The wider set of beliefs the Duat sits inside, and the reason each
      funerary text depicts it differently.
