The !!Duat!! or !!Tuat!! ([[ Ancient Egyptian | ancient-egypt ]]: [[ Hieroglyph | egyptian-hieroglyphs ]]: 𓇽 romanized: [[ dwꜣt | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dwꜣt#Egyptian ]]) is a concept in ancient [[ Egyptian mythology ]] involving death, and it is most often seen as a realm where people go after they die. Linguistic shifts within [[ Ancient Egypt ]] left it with other names as well: the //Duat// has also been called !!Te!! (Coptic: Ⲧⲏ, romanized: //Tē//) and !!Amenthes!! (Ancient Greek: Ἀμένθης, [[ romanized | romanization-of-ancient-greek ]]: //Aménthēs//).

What is known of the //Duat// derives principally from funerary texts: the //[[ Book of Gates ]]//, the //[[ Book of Caverns ]]//, the //[[ Coffin Texts ]]//, the //[[ Amduat ]]//, and the //[[ Book of the Dead ]],// among many other sources.[[ ^2 ]][[ ^3 ]] From them it is generally known best as a dark subterranean realm that houses not only the deceased, but a variety of [[ deities | deity ]].[[ ^4 ]] The ones that turn up most often in these texts are [[ Osiris ]], [[ Anubis ]], [[ Thoth ]], [[ Horus ]], and [[ Maat ]], in various forms.[[ ^1 ]] All of these documents involve the //Duat//, but each of them fulfilled a different [[ purpose | ancient-egyptian-afterlife-beliefs ]] and depicts the //Duat// in a variety of unique ways.[[ ^5 ]][[ ^6 ]]

== Overview

Two things happen in the //Duat// more than anything else: the soul is judged after death, and the sun god [[ Ra ]] is reborn each night. It is depicted most often as the setting for those rituals and [[ mythological | egyptian-mythology ]] events.[[ ^7 ]] The realm is divided by [[ twelve guarded gates | gate-deities-of-the-underworld ]], one for each hour of the night, closely associated with Ra's journey, and the sections they mark prominently feature [[ Osiris ]], god of the //Duat// and personification of rebirth.[[ ^8 ]]

== Geography

A burial chamber was a touching-point, the place where the mundane world and the //Duat// met.[[ ^9 ]] That is why the west bank of the [[ Nile ]] was associated with the dead, and why [[ funeral barges | ancient-egyptian-funerary-practices ]] would mimic the sun god Ra's journey through the sky during the day.[[ ^10 ]]

The country on the other side is described with many realistic features: rivers, islands, fields, paths and lakes. Texts also describe lakes of fire, walls of iron, and trees of turquoise.[[ ^2 ]] These lands are dark, partitioned with a series of gates, and mostly identified as Shetit, a dangerous land translated as 'The Beyond'.[[ ^4 ]] The other titles run the same way: 'The Hidden Place', described as a region where mortals and the divine are absent;[[ ^4 ]] and 'Road of the Secret Things of Re-stau', a road that Ra passes in his nightly journey.[[ ^7 ]]

For the deceased, the important address is 'The Hall of Truth', often depicted alongside the '[[ Weighing of the Heart ]]' ritual in //Books of the Dead// and mentioned in the sixth hour of Ra's journey.[[ ^11 ]][[ ^7 ]] The dead found virtuous are permitted to dwell in [[ //A'aru// ]], also known as 'The Field of Reeds'.[[ ^12 ]] It is described as a paradise where the dead could live their former life with their loved ones without pain or suffering. Harvest of crops is often featured in depictions of //A'aru//, because the harvests were believed never to be poor and the land to be in eternal spring.[[ ^12 ]]

== Deities

There are hundreds of different divinites of varying importance related to the //Duat// throughout time. The most referenced are these.

- [[ Osiris ]], believed to be the lord of the realm, who personifies rebirth and the afterlife. In [[ his own mythology | osiris-myth ]] he is himself reborn after his brother [[ Seth | set-deity ]] slays him and his wife [[ Isis ]] partially revives him. He is depicted as a man with green skin partially wrapped in bandages.[[ ^13 ]]
- [[ Anubis ]], son of Osiris and [[ Nephthys ]], who weighs the heart of the deceased. His domains also cover the [[ embalming ]] and mummification process, and he guides souls to the //Duat// upon death. He is depicted with the black head of a canine, most often assumed to be a [[ jackal ]].[[ ^14 ]]
- [[ Thoth ]], who records the verdict of the 'Weighing of the Heart'. He is most often depicted as an [[ Ibis ]] headed man with a stylus and tablet.[[ ^14 ]]
- [[ Horus ]], son of Osiris and Isis, who protects Ra during his journey through the //Duat// and is heir to Osiris.[[ ^14 ]]
- [[ Ma'at ]], who is both the goddess of order and the conception of order, balance and truth itself. She is the feather that is weighed against the heart in the 'Weighing of the Heart' ritual.[[ ^14 ]]
- [[ Ammit ]], who eats the hearts and souls of those who cannot pass the 'Weighing of the Heart' ritual. She is often depicted near the scales in //Books of the Dead// with the face of a crocodile, the mane and front half of a lion, and the hindquarters of a hippopotamus.[[ ^14 ]]
- [[ Ra ]], god of the sun, who must journey through the //Duat// every night to be reborn every morning.[[ ^14 ]]
- [[ Apep ]], the personification of darkness and true chaos, who attempts to devour Ra and bring about eternal chaos.[[ ^14 ]]

== Mythological significance



== Housing of souls and afterlife

Judgement had to be travelled to. The dead journeyed through the various parts of the Duat to be judged, and if they were able to pass various challenges they reached the //Judgment of the dead//. The first task in that ritual was to address each of the forty-two [[ Assessors of Maat ]] correctly by name, while reciting the sins they did not commit during their lifetime.[[ ^15 ]] After confirming that they were sinless, the heart of the deceased was weighed by [[ Anubis ]] against the feather of [[ Maat ]], which represents truth and justice. Any heart heavier than the feather failed the test, and was rejected and eaten by [[ Ammit ]], the devourer of souls; those people were denied existence after death in the Duat. A soul lighter than the feather passed this most important test, and would be allowed to travel to Aaru.

The //Duat// is not equivalent to the conceptions of [[ Hell ]] in the [[ Abrahamic religions ]], in which souls are condemned with fiery torment. The absolute punishment for the wicked, in ancient Egyptian thought, was the denial of an afterlife to the deceased: ceasing to exist in the intellectual form, which is what the devouring of the heart by Ammit meant.[[ ^9 ]]

== Journey of the Sun

Each night Ra travelled through the //Duat//, and the main benefit to the dead was revivification. In the underworld he was in his [[ ram | ovis-longipes-palaeoaegyptiacus ]]-headed form, Af. He travelled under the world upon his [[ Atet ]] barge from west to east, and in the course of the underground journey he was transformed from his aged [[ Atum ]] form into his young [[ Khepri ]] form, the new dawning sun. The role of the dead king, worshiped as a god, was also central to the mythology surrounding the concept of Duat, and he is often depicted as being identical with Ra.[[ ^16 ]]

The dead king travelled through the Duat, the Kingdom of Osiris, along with the sun god, using the special knowledge he was supposed to possess. That knowledge was recorded in the [[ Coffin Texts ]], which served as a guide to the hereafter not just for the king but for all deceased. According to the //[[ Amduat ]]//, the underworld consists of twelve regions signifying the twelve hours of the sun god's journey through it, battling [[ Apep ]] in order to bring order back to the earth in the morning. As his rays illuminated the Duat during the journey, they revived the dead who occupied the underworld and let them enjoy life after death during that hour of the night when they were in the presence of the sun god, after which they resumed their sleep, waiting for the god's return the following night.[[ ^5 ]]

== Differences in sources

The //Duat// has a variety of depictions, but the //[[ Book of the Dead ]]// (also known as The Book of Coming Forth by Day) is considered to be an important and vital resource to understanding it, and is one of the most studied texts in Egyptology.[[ ^1 ]][[ ^11 ]] The conceptions of the //Duat// found in that text are therefore the most commonly referenced [[ canon | canon-fiction ]] when discussing [[ ancient Egyptian afterlife beliefs ]]. The //[[ Book of the Dead ]]// and the [[ Coffin Texts ]] were collections of spells and prayers to protect the dead from the //Duat//'s dangerous landscape. None of the available sources that discuss the Duat are to lay out a geography or coherent setting, but to describe a succession of rites of passage which the dead would have to pass to reach eternal life.[[ ^5 ]]

== In popular culture

- The Duat appears as a major location in [[ Rick Riordan ]]'s //[[ The Kane Chronicles ]]// series. It is revealed in //[[ The Crown of Ptolemy ]]// to have connections to the Mist from Riordan's //[[ Camp Half-Blood Chronicles ]]// series.
- In //[[ Assassin's Creed Origins ]]//, the Duat is an important aspect of the game, with the main character, [[ Bayek ]], entering it multiple times.
- The Duat makes an appearance in the episode "[[ Asylum | asylum-moon-knight ]]" of the [[ Disney+ ]] series //[[ Moon Knight | moon-knight-miniseries ]]// (2022), as the place where [[ Marc Spector | marc-spector-marvel-cinematic-universe ]] and his alter [[ Steven Grant | steven-grant-marvel-cinematic-universe ]] are guided by the Egyptian goddess [[ Taweret | taweret-marvel-cinematic-universe ]] in the afterlife after being killed by [[ Arthur Harrow ]].[[ ^17 ]]
- In seminal Beat Generation author William S. Burroughs' work //[[ The Western Lands ]]//, the time travelling, anti hero gunslinger Kim Carsons explores the Duat and confronts its inhabitants.[[ ^18 ]]

